---
import InfoPageLayout from '../layouts/InfoPageLayout.astro';

const title = 'How to Play Mystery Emblem | Pokemon Fire Emblem Fan Game Guide';
const description =
  'Learn the basics of Mystery Emblem: moving on the grid, attacking and counterattacks, Pokemon types, status cures, healing, recruiting, training, and planning before each chapter.';
---

<InfoPageLayout title={title} description={description} heading="How to Play">
  <p>
    Mystery Emblem teaches everything as you go through Quaquaval's Prologue training bout and
    <em>Quaquaval's Field Notes</em>, so you can jump straight in. This page is a quick reference if
    you want to review the rules ahead of time.
  </p>

  <h2>Taking turns</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Your turn:</strong> tap one of your Pokemon. Blue squares show where it can move, and red squares show what it can attack.</li>
    <li>Tap a blue square to move, then choose <strong>Attack</strong>, a heal, an item, or <strong>Wait</strong>.</li>
    <li>Once all your Pokemon have acted, the <strong>enemy's turn</strong> begins and they move and attack.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    Your hero, the Dragon you received from the personality quiz, must stay standing. If your hero
    falls, the battle is lost. Other allies who faint recover after the battle.
  </p>

  <h2>Attacking and Loadouts</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Physical moves</strong> hit an enemy right next to you (Range 1). <strong>Special moves</strong> hit from two squares away (Range 2).</li>
    <li>Before you attack, a forecast shows damage, hit chance, type effectiveness, and whether the enemy can counterattack.</li>
    <li>Each Pokemon carries up to four moves and marks up to one Physical and one Special move <strong>[E]</strong> for Enemy Phase counters. Striking from a distance where the foe has no matching [E] move lets you attack safely without a counter!</li>
    <li>If your Pokemon's Speed exceeds its target's by 5 or more, it strikes twice.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Healing, Status, and Items</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Healers like Flabebe restore an ally's HP from 1 to 2 squares away.</li>
    <li>Status conditions like Paralysis, Burn, Poison, Sleep, and Freeze hinder units in battle; remedies such as Paralyze Heal and Full Heal cure them on your turn.</li>
    <li>Passive held berries trigger automatically at low HP, while glowing Wonder Tiles heal HP and clear status at the start of your phase.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The battlefield</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Forests and mountains slow Grounded units down while granting Dodge and Defense cover.</li>
    <li>Water blocks most Grounded Pokemon, while Flying, Levitate, and Water types cross it freely.</li>
    <li>Enemy reinforcements can climb onto the map from Stairs; stand on Stairs to block them.</li>
    <li>Objectives vary by chapter: rout the enemy squad, rescue an ally, guide your leader to an exit, or defeat a Rift boss.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Growing your team</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Recruit:</strong> new companions join across the story and side chapters, and hesitant allies like Slakoth can be woken mid-battle with <strong>Talk</strong>.</li>
    <li><strong>Level up:</strong> combat and support actions grant EXP; veteran mentors like Quaquaval earn reduced EXP so younger recruits can catch up quickly.</li>
    <li><strong>Grow:</strong> Act 1 keeps every Pokemon in its base form; strengthen your team with vitamins, held gear, and TMs.</li>
    <li><strong>Shop:</strong> Kecleon's roadside stalls appear on cleared world map nodes starting in Chapter 2, stocking medicine, gear, X-Items, and TMs.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Before each chapter</h2>
  <p>
    Starting in Chapter 2, Battle Prep lets you pick who deploys, swap starting tiles on the map,
    customize 4-move loadouts, and hand out Convoy items before tapping Fight!
  </p>

  <h2>Saving</h2>
  <p>
    The game saves automatically to your browser. Sign in with Google from the title screen to sync
    your campaign across phone and PC. More questions? See the <a href="/faq/">FAQ</a>.
  </p>
</InfoPageLayout>
